"49_cocoa.mst — the Cocoa bridge, C0 (docs/cocoa_bridge_design.md), MACDART
 BINDING. The API is MACVM's; the substance is dart:cocoa's typed
 objc_msgSend marshaller — ONE bridge under both languages (ST_PLAN.md
 Sprint 13). An ObjcRef holds the Dart-side `Cocoa` wrapper in an ivar, so
 retain/release policy, weak-persistent finalizers, ownership families
 (alloc/new/copy/init results arrive already-owned) and result wrapping
 live in exactly one place, shared with everything dartui itself draws.

   | pi |
   pi := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSProcessInfo') send: 'processInfo'.
   Transcript showCr: (pi sendString: 'processName').
   pi release.

 Ownership (MACDART): every wrapped result is retained by the Dart wrapper,
 which releases when the wrapper is collected. `release` POISONS this
 ObjcRef — later sends fail cleanly, like a terminated worker — and the
 native reference drops at the wrapper's collection: a deliberate
 divergence from MACVM's immediate release, in the safe direction (an
 over-release corrupts Cocoa; a deferred one merely lingers). Use
 `Cocoa poolDo: [...]` around allocation bursts. A caught NSException
 surfaces as an ordinary Smalltalk error — the VM never dies. In a headless
 host (the plain `dart` CLI) main-thread sends fail cleanly: there is no
 run loop draining the main queue."
"ObjcMainProxy — the C3 ergonomic face of the main-thread hop: `ref onMain`
 answers a proxy whose every send runs the SAME Objective-C message on the
 MAIN thread, synchronously (docs/cocoa_bridge_design.md §4 path 2 —
 AppKit is main-thread-only). Defined before ObjcRef so `onMain` can
 reference the global; its own body needs no forward names (sends are
 late-bound). Only meaningful in a GUI host whose run loop drains the main
 queue — headless, every send through it fails cleanly."
"I am a main-thread proxy on an ObjcRef: every message sent to me runs the
 same Objective-C message on the process main thread, synchronously, and
 answers the result. AppKit is main-thread-only, so use me for all window
 and view work: `window onMain makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil`. Obtain me with
 ObjcRef>>onMain; `target` answers the underlying ObjcRef for wrapper
 operations such as release and isValid."
Object subclass: ObjcMainProxy [
    | ref |
    ObjcMainProxy class >> on: anObjcRef [
        | p |
        p := self basicNew.
        p setRef: anObjcRef.
        ^p
    ]
    setRef: r [
        ref := r
    ]
    target [
        "The underlying ObjcRef (for release/isValid, which are wrapper
         operations, not sends)."
        ^ref
    ]
    doesNotUnderstand: aMessage [
        ^ref sendMain: aMessage selector asString args: aMessage arguments
    ]
]

"STCocoa — the primitive floor of this binding: each method is a direct
 call into a dart:cocoa helper (the <stprim:> door — the same mechanism
 the prelude's own primitives use). Everything above it is ordinary
 Smalltalk."
Object subclass: STCocoa [
    STCocoa class >> classNamed: aName [ <stprim: stObjcClassNamed> ]
    STCocoa class >> send: h sel: s args: a [ <stprim: stObjcSend> ]
    STCocoa class >> sendMain: h sel: s args: a [ <stprim: stObjcSendMain> ]
    STCocoa class >> isRef: x [ <stprim: stObjcIsRef> ]
    STCocoa class >> utf8: x [ <stprim: stObjcUTF8> ]
    STCocoa class >> poolPush [ <stprim: stObjcPoolPush> ]
    STCocoa class >> poolPop: p [ <stprim: stObjcPoolPop> ]
    STCocoa class >> actionTargetTicket: t [ <stprim: stObjcActionTarget> ]
    STCocoa class >> tableSourceTicket: t [ <stprim: stObjcTableSource> ]
    STCocoa class >> joinRows: anArray [ <stprim: stJoinRows> ]
]

"I am a Smalltalk handle on one Objective-C object, minted only by the
 bridge (Cocoa classNamed:, Cocoa nsString:, and the results of sends).
 Any message I do not implement myself becomes an Objective-C send — the
 selector text is identical in both languages, the argument and result
 shapes are resolved from the live runtime's own @encode (a SmallInteger
 passed where a double is declared coerces correctly), and results come
 back as ObjcRefs, Booleans, Integers, Doubles, Strings, or small Arrays
 of numbers for the common structs (a CGRect argument is its four Doubles
 in an Array). Typed sends (sendString:, sendI64:, sendBool:, ...) pin the
 return interpretation. `onMain` answers a proxy that runs every send on
 the main thread (required for AppKit). After `release` I am poisoned and
 further sends fail cleanly.

 COLLISIONS: selectors I really implement never reach Objective-C —
 notably `class` (escape: objcClass), `printString` (the ObjC description
 via my printOn:), `release` (the wrapper's own), `isValid` (the poison
 check, NOT NSTimer's -isValid), and `=`/`==`, which compare WRAPPER
 identity — two ObjcRefs on one object are not `=`; use `isEqual:` for
 Objective-C equality. Manual reference counting (retain/autorelease/
 dealloc/retainCount) is refused outright: ownership belongs to the
 bridge (wrap/release/poolDo:), never to guest code."
Object subclass: ObjcRef [
    | h |
    ObjcRef class >> onHandle: aDartCocoa [
        | r |
        r := self basicNew.
        r setHandle: aDartCocoa.
        ^r
    ]
    setHandle: aDartCocoa [
        h := aDartCocoa
    ]
    objcHandle [
        "The Dart `Cocoa` wrapper this ref rides on (the bridge unwraps
         ObjcRef arguments through this accessor)."
        ^h
    ]
    wrapResult: x [
        "Object results arrive as Dart wrappers — mint an ObjcRef so the
         Smalltalk side stays in Smalltalk types; scalars pass through."
        (STCocoa isRef: x) ifTrue: [ ^ObjcRef onHandle: x ].
        ^x
    ]

    "── the C1 general send ──────────────────────────────────────────────
     Arguments may be nil, true/false, Integers, Doubles, Strings (bridged
     to a temp NSString), Arrays of numbers (struct-by-value: an NSRange is
     two Integers, a CGPoint two Doubles, a CGRect four Doubles), or
     ObjcRefs. MACVM pinned return shapes with a retToken; this bridge
     resolves the shape from the method's own @encode, so the ret: variants
     keep their signatures with the token advisory."
    send: selName [
        "Send an Objective-C message (no arguments)."
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {})
    ]
    send: selName with: a [
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: { a })
    ]
    send: selName with: a with: b [
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: { a. b })
    ]
    send: selName args: argArray [
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: argArray)
    ]
    send: selName args: argArray ret: retToken [
        ^self send: selName args: argArray
    ]
    sendI64: selName [ ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {} ]
    sendBool: selName [ ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {} ]
    sendBool: selName args: argArray [
        ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: argArray
    ]
    sendF64: selName [ ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {} ]
    sendF64: selName args: argArray [
        ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: argArray
    ]
    sendString: selName [
        "The result as a Smalltalk String (an NSString result is read out
         through UTF8String)."
        ^ STCocoa utf8: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {})
    ]
    sendRange: selName args: argArray [
        ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: argArray
    ]
    sendPoint: selName [ ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {} ]
    sendRect: selName [ ^ STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: {} ]

    "── C2: doesNotUnderstand: dispatch ──────────────────────────────────
     Any Smalltalk unary/keyword send I don't natively answer becomes the
     Objective-C message — see the class comment's COLLISIONS note.

       calendar := (Cocoa classNamed: 'NSCalendar') currentCalendar.
       s := ((Cocoa classNamed: 'NSMutableString') alloc) init.
       s appendString: 'hello'."
    doesNotUnderstand: aMessage [
        | sel |
        sel := aMessage selector asString.
        ((sel = 'retain') | (sel = 'autorelease') | (sel = 'dealloc')
            | (sel = 'retainCount')) ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'Cocoa: manual reference counting belongs to the bridge, not guest code' ].
        ^self primSendAuto: sel args: aMessage arguments
    ]
    primSendAuto: selName args: argArray [
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa send: h sel: selName args: argArray)
    ]
    objcClass [
        "The #class collision escape: the receiver's OBJECTIVE-C class
         (Smalltalk `class` answers my class value, as it must)."
        ^self send: 'class'
    ]

    "── C3: the sync main-thread hop ─────────────────────────────────────
     AppKit is main-thread-only; these run the send ON the main thread and
     wait. In a headless host the hop fails cleanly — there is no run loop
     to drain the queue. Already-on-main callers run inline."
    primSendMainAuto: selName args: argArray [
        ^self wrapResult: (STCocoa sendMain: h sel: selName args: argArray)
    ]
    sendMain: selName args: argArray [
        "One send on the main thread, shape auto-resolved like sendAuto."
        ^self primSendMainAuto: selName args: argArray
    ]
    onMain [
        "A proxy that runs EVERY send on the main thread:
           window onMain makeKeyAndOrderFront: nil."
        ^ObjcMainProxy on: self
    ]
    copy [
        "Objective-C -copy (+1 family — the wrapper owns the result).
         Defined EXPLICITLY so a future Object>>copy can never shadow it."
        ^self send: 'copy'
    ]
    release [
        "Poison this ref: later sends fail cleanly. The native reference
         drops when the Dart wrapper is collected (see the class comment)."
        h := nil
    ]
    isValid [
        ^h notNil
    ]
    descriptionString [
        ^ STCocoa utf8: (STCocoa send: h sel: 'description' args: {})
    ]
    printOn: ws [
        ws nextPutAll: (h isNil
            ifTrue: [ 'ObjcRef(released)' ]
            ifFalse: [ self descriptionString ])
    ]
]

"Cocoa poolDo: [...] — an autorelease pool around a burst of bridge work.
 keep: answers its argument (the Dart wrapper owns the reference; nothing
 extra to retain here) — kept for MACVM source compatibility."
Object subclass: CocoaPool [
    CocoaPool class >> new [ ^self basicNew ]
    keep: anObjcRef [ ^anObjcRef ]
]

"The class-side facade: classNamed:, nsString:, and pool scoping."
Object subclass: Cocoa [
    Cocoa class >> classNamed: aName [
        | c |
        c := STCocoa classNamed: aName.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^ObjcRef onHandle: c
    ]
    Cocoa class >> nsString: aString [
        "A retained NSString from a Smalltalk String."
        ^(self classNamed: 'NSString')
            send: 'stringWithUTF8String:' with: aString
    ]
    Cocoa class >> drainPool [
        "MACVM drained its bottom pool here; this host drains per event —
         a no-op kept for source compatibility."
        ^self
    ]
    Cocoa class >> poolDo: aBlock [
        | p |
        p := STCocoa poolPush.
        ^[ aBlock value ] ensure: [ STCocoa poolPop: p ]
    ]
    Cocoa class >> primPoolPush [ ^ STCocoa poolPush ]
    Cocoa class >> primPoolPop: p [ ^ STCocoa poolPop: p ]
]
